  }],
  x: { min: 0, max: 32, label: 'orbit size a (AU)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 170, label: 'period T (years)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 360,
};

const scaleCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 's', label: 'model Sun diameter', min: 5, max: 50, step: 1, value: 22, unit: 'cm' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'Earth–Sun distance', expr: '1.075 * s', unit: 'm' },
    { label: 'Neptune–Sun distance', expr: '32.2 * s', unit: 'm' },
    { label: 'Earth diameter', expr: '0.0916 * s', unit: 'mm' },
    { label: 'distance to nearest star', expr: '288 * s', unit: 'km' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/foundational/solar-system-scale">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The solar system is mostly emptiness with a lighting problem. Space is so vast that honest
    diagrams are impossible — any picture that shows Earth and the Sun at a sensible size has
    already lied about the distance between them by a factor of thousands. Astronomy's solution
    is a natural yardstick: the <strong>astronomical unit (AU)</strong>, defined as the average
    Sun–Earth distance, 149.6 million km. On this scale the numbers stay human-ish: Mercury
    orbits at 0.4 AU, Jupiter at 5.2, Neptune at 30.
  </p>
  <p>
    Light, the fastest thing there is, needs <strong>8 minutes 20 seconds</strong> to cross one
    AU — so the Sun you see is always 8 minutes old. That lag grows with distance until it
    becomes the defining fact of exploration: the Moon is 1.3 light-seconds away, Mars 3–22
    light-minutes depending on where both planets are, Neptune over four light-hours, and
    Voyager 1 — launched in 1977, now more than 160 AU out — about 23 light-hours, so a radio
    command takes nearly two days to make the round trip. The solar system is best imagined as
    a slow-motion conversation conducted at the speed of light.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="orbital period against distance — the curve is Kepler's third law; the dots are the actual planets" widget="plotter" config={keplerCfg} />

  <p>
    Hover the dots: every planet sits on the curve, from Mercury (88 days) to Neptune (165
    years). One power law, discovered in 1619 from naked-eye data, survived Newton, relativity
    and 5,000+ exoplanet discoveries. Distance and period are the solar system's two honest
    coordinates — everything else is detail.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring the yardstick: how we found the AU</h2>
  <p>
    For two millennia nobody knew the scale — Greek estimates of the Sun's distance were about
    twenty times too small, and Copernicus inherited the error. The breakthrough was
    <strong>parallax</strong>: view an object from two distant places and its apparent shift
    against the background reveals its distance by simple trigonometry. In 1672 Giovanni Cassini
    and Jean Richer measured Mars from Paris and Cayenne simultaneously, 7,000 km apart, and the
    tiny angular shift gave the Mars distance — from which the Sun–Earth distance followed by
    Kepler's geometry. They landed within about 7% of the truth: the first credible number for
    the scale of the solar system.
  </p>
  <p>
    The refinement came from <strong>transits of Venus</strong>, when Venus crosses the Sun's
    face as a black dot. Edmond Halley realised in 1716 that observers stationed worldwide would
    each time the transit slightly differently, and the differences would pin the AU down
    precisely. The 1761 and 1769 expeditions were the great international science projects of
    their age — including Captain Cook sailing to Tahiti for the 1769 event — and shrank the
    error to about 1%. Today the AU is no longer measured but <em>defined</em>: since 2012 it is
    exactly 149,597,870,700 metres, fixed by international agreement now that radar timing gives
    distances to metres. (Radar itself made the 1960s measurement: bounce pulses off Venus and
    time the echo.)
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the AU is not "the Earth–Sun distance today". Earth's orbit
  is an ellipse, so our actual distance swings between about 147.1 and 152.1 million km across
  the year; the AU is the orbit's semi-major axis — an average, now a fixed constant by
  definition.</p>

  <h2>A tour by distance</h2>
  <p>
    Set out from the Sun and the geography is relentlessly repetitive: <em>rock</em>, then
    <em>nothing</em>, then <em>gas</em>, then <em>ice</em>, then <em>nothing again</em>. The four
    inner planets — Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars — are small, dense worlds of rock and metal
    within 1.5 AU. At 2–3 AU drifts the <strong>asteroid belt</strong>: millions of rocky leftovers
    whose combined mass is under 4% of our Moon's — a failed planet, kept from forming by
    Jupiter's gravitational bullying. Then the <strong>gas giants</strong> Jupiter (11 Earth
    diameters wide, 318 Earth masses) and Saturn, followed by the <strong>ice giants</strong>
    Uranus and Neptune at 19 and 30 AU. Beyond Neptune, from about 30 to 50 AU, spreads the
    <strong>Kuiper Belt</strong> — a doughnut of icy bodies including Pluto and Eris, the
    solar system's second population of "planets" that nobody expected. And a thousand times
    farther still, somewhere between a few thousand and perhaps 100,000 AU, lies the
    <strong>Oort Cloud</strong>: a spherical shell of a trillion comet nuclei, never directly
    observed, inferred entirely from the orbits of long-period comets that fall sunward from
    random directions. The map still has blank edges.
  </p>

  <h2>Feels-like scale</h2>
  <p>
    Numbers in AU slide off the brain, so shrink the system instead: make the Sun a football,
    22 cm across. Earth becomes a 2 mm grain — a peppercorn — orbiting 23 metres away. Jupiter
    is a marble at 120 metres; Neptune orbits 700 metres out; and on this scale the nearest
    star, Proxima Centauri, is another football roughly 6,300 km away — about the distance from
    London to New York, with almost nothing in between. The panel below is the same machine,
    adjustable: set any model Sun size and read off what everything else must become.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the scale machine — pick a model Sun size and read off the true emptiness" widget="sliderLab" config={scaleCfg} />

  <p>
    Note what the machine quietly demonstrates: the <strong>planets are specks</strong>. All
    eight worlds together hold under 0.2% of the solar system's mass; the Sun holds the other
    99.8%+. The familiar classroom poster, with planets lined up shoulder to shoulder, shows
    their <em>order</em> and nothing else — at true scale, on a football-Sun model, every
    planet in the picture would be invisible without a microscope.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the planets are built the way they are</h2>
  <p>
    The rocky-inner/gassy-outer split is not an accident of taste but the signature of how the
    system formed, about 4.6 billion years ago. A cloud of gas and dust collapsed into a
    spinning disk with the young Sun at its centre. Near the Sun it was hot: only rock and metal
    could condense, so only small, dense worlds could grow. Past the <em>frost line</em>, near
    3 AU, water, methane and ammonia froze into abundant ices, and planetary embryos could bulk
    up to several Earth masses of solid material — big enough to grab and hold hydrogen and
    helium from the disk, growing into the gas giants. Farther out, the disk was thinner and
    growth slower, leaving the ice giants partially formed. Leftovers never swept up became the
    asteroids (rocky, inside the frost line) and the comets (icy, outside). Every world is a
    receipt from this shopping trip: what could condense where, and what got grabbed by whom.
  </p>

  <h2>Comets, meteoroids and rare alignments</h2>
  <p>
    The small bodies have their own vocabulary, and exams enjoy testing it. A
    <strong>meteoroid</strong> is the grain in space; the streak of light as it burns in our
    atmosphere is a <strong>meteor</strong> (a "shooting star", though no star is involved); a
    fragment that survives to the ground is a <strong>meteorite</strong>. Meteor
    <em>showers</em> happen when Earth ploughs through the dust trail left by a comet — the
    Perseids every August are debris of comet Swift–Tuttle. A comet near the Sun grows two
    tails, and the ion tail has a famous habit: it always points <em>away</em> from the Sun,
    blown straight back by the outflowing solar wind, regardless of the comet's direction of
    travel — a free windsock for a wind you cannot see.
  </p>
  <p>
    Transits of Venus rewarded observers with more than the AU: they are genuinely rare,
    occurring in pairs eight years apart separated by more than a century — the last pair in
    2004 and 2012, the next in 2117. And they tie into an open question about our own planet:
    where Earth's water came from. Comets were long assumed, but spacecraft that sampled comet
    ice (Rosetta at 67P, 2014) found its hydrogen fingerprint fails to match ocean water, while
    carbon-rich asteroids match it well — shifting suspicion to the asteroid belt. The solar
    system's scale, its inventory and its history are still being read off the same rocks.
  </p>

  <h2>The definition fight: what counts as a planet?</h2>
  <p>
    The scale model above also explains the solar system's most famous administrative
    decision. When surveys of the Kuiper Belt started turning up bodies comparable to Pluto
    — Eris is slightly denser and nearly as wide — astronomers faced a choice: an ever-growing
    roster of planets, or a definition. The International Astronomical Union's 2006 ruling
    requires three things of a planet: it orbits the Sun, it is massive enough for gravity
    to pull it round (hydrostatic equilibrium), and it has <em>cleared its orbital
    neighbourhood</em>. Pluto fails the third — it lives inside the Kuiper Belt's crowd — so
    it is a <strong>dwarf planet</strong>, alongside Eris, Ceres (in the asteroid belt),
    Haumea and Makemake. The decision was controversial and remains argued about, but the
    logic was really the scale lesson: <em>where you live is part of what you are</em>, and
    at 30–50 AU you live in a swarm.
  </p>
  <p>
    Exploration, meanwhile, has been checking the map at steadily faster cruise speeds. It
    took <em>Pioneer 10</em> 21 months to reach Jupiter (1973); <em>Voyager 2</em> used a
    once-in-176-years planetary alignment to tour all four giant planets (1977–89);
    <em>New Horizons</em> crossed the 30+ AU to Pluto in 9½ years (2015), photographing
    nitrogen glaciers on a body everyone had known only as a pixelated dot; and the
    <em>Parker Solar Probe</em> now holds the speed record — over 190 km/s at perihelion,
    falling past the Sun faster than any human object ever built. Each mission's flight
    path is Kepler's laws, executed with mid-course corrections.
  </p>

  <h2>Even the crowds are empty</h2>
  <p>
    Two scale facts complete the emptiness theme. The asteroid belt, drawn in every picture
    as a congested ring, is in reality a dusting: its million-plus catalogued asteroids are
    spread over an orbital annulus with a volume so vast that typical separations run to
    millions of kilometres — spacecraft cross the belt aimed through it, not around it, and
    the chance of hitting anything is negligible. And within each planet's own neighbourhood
    the same holds: every world keeps a clear lane around it (that is, remember, part of the
    planet definition), and its moons are separated by gulfs measured in planet-widths. The
    solar system is not a clockwork of touching spheres but a few grains of matter spread
    over a hall the size of a county — with, orbiting between them, only sunlight and the
    occasional speck of a spacecraft.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The measurement chain that built the AU is a case study in how science stacks. Parallax
    needs a baseline; Earth's diameter was the first baseline (Eratosthenes measured it to a
    few percent around 240 BCE); Cassini's intercontinental baselines extended it to Mars; the
    transit expeditions extended it to Venus; radar (1961 onward) replaced angles with
    time-of-flight; and spacecraft telemetry now tracks distances to metres. Each generation
    reused the previous one's constants — the way you get from a Greek shadow-stick to a
    Voyager antenna is one audited link at a time.
  </p>
  <p>
    The scale also disciplines speculation. "All the planets will align" headlines appear
    regularly, but true alignment never happens: the orbits are tilted a few degrees relative
    to each other and the planets are light-minutes to light-hours apart, so even the best
    groupings leave them strung across a wide patch of sky. Gravitational tugs during
    line-ups are similarly overrated — at their closest approach, Jupiter pulls Earth with a
    force about 1/30,000th of the Moon's. And the frontier is real: the Kuiper Belt's very
    existence was doubted until 1992, when the first object (1992 QB1) was found beyond
    Neptune; since then over 3,000 have been catalogued, and the clustering of some extreme
    orbits has revived the "Planet Nine" hypothesis — an unseen world perhaps 5–10 Earth
    masses, hundreds of AU out, that nobody has yet imaged. The blank edges of the map are
    still being surveyed.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NASA planetary fact sheets; IAU 2012 resolution B2 (definition of the astronomical unit); Kepler, <em>Harmonices Mundi</em> (1619); JPL Voyager mission status; Rosetta/67P D/H results (2015).</p>
</TopicPage>
